12:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section battle-flow-section","id":"battle-flow","children":["$","$L1e",null,{"guide":{"date":"July 1–August 3, 1862","place":"Malvern Hill and Harrison’s Landing on the James River, Virginia; Washington, D.C.; and the planned transfer route through Fort Monroe to Aquia Creek, Virginia","result":"The Army of the Potomac won the defensive battle at Malvern Hill but continued its withdrawal to Harrison’s Landing rather than counterattacking. McClellan then asked for major reinforcements to renew operations against Richmond. After consulting him, General-in-Chief Henry W. Halleck concluded that Washington could not supply the requested force and ordered the army transferred from the Peninsula to Aquia Creek to unite with John Pope’s Army of Virginia","facts":[{"label":"Episode scope","value":"The aftermath of Malvern Hill, the Harrison’s Landing encampment, and the decision to end the Peninsula Campaign","note":"The opening section reviews responsibility for the July 1 battle before the narrative follows McClellan, Lincoln, and Halleck through August 3."},{"label":"Malvern Hill result","value":"United States victory on July 1, 1862","note":"Confederate attacks were poorly coordinated and failed to break the Federal position. The National Park Service estimates about 3,000 United States and 5,355 Confederate casualties."},{"label":"Confederate attack pattern","value":"Separate brigades entered the fight without a single coordinated assault","note":"The episode accepts D. H. Hill’s criticism that the attacks were piecemeal but rejects his claim that Magruder’s command did little fighting."},{"label":"Magruder’s participation","value":"Three of four brigades made substantial attacks","note":"The episode states that Magruder’s men reached closer to the Federal line near the Crew house than other Confederate formations."},{"label":"Episode’s responsibility assessment","value":"Robert E. Lee held final command responsibility for the failed attacks","note":"This is the hosts’ interpretation. They emphasize Lee’s approval of the artillery plan, the ambiguous attack order, his failure to cancel it, and his later orders to continue the assault."},{"label":"Withdrawal after Malvern Hill","value":"About eight miles to Harrison’s Landing on July 2","note":"McClellan declined proposals for a counterattack and moved the army down the James River to an entrenched position protected by United States gunboats."},{"label":"Army at Harrison’s Landing","value":"About 100,000 soldiers in the U.S. Army campaign summary","note":"The army occupied a strong bridgehead on the James. Disease, heat, logistics, and the separation from Pope’s army shaped the administration’s later decision."},{"label":"McClellan’s enemy estimate","value":"Nearly 180,000 Confederates in the episode’s account","note":"The episode presents this as a serious overestimate that reinforced McClellan’s belief that withdrawal was necessary."},{"label":"Field command criticism","value":"The episode faults McClellan for remaining away from key fighting","note":"The hosts distinguish their explanation—protection of his reputation—from either personal cowardice or treason. That explanation is interpretation, not an established documentary fact."},{"label":"Lincoln at Harrison’s Landing","value":"July 7, 1862","note":"Lincoln traveled to inspect the army and met McClellan. The Library of Congress dates McClellan’s Harrison’s Landing letter to the president to the same day."},{"label":"Harrison’s Landing letter","value":"Military strategy and a conciliatory war policy","note":"McClellan argued that the war should preserve the Union without becoming a campaign of subjugation, confiscation, or forcible abolition. The episode describes the letter as unsolicited advice extending beyond field command."},{"label":"Second Confiscation Act","value":"Approved July 17, 1862","note":"The legislation expanded Federal action against rebel property and addressed the freedom of enslaved people held by participants in the rebellion."},{"label":"First emancipation draft presented","value":"July 22, 1862","note":"Lincoln read his preliminary draft to the cabinet as a military measure. He delayed public issuance until after a favorable military event; the preliminary proclamation followed Antietam in September."},{"label":"New general-in-chief","value":"Henry W. Halleck, appointed July 11, 1862","note":"The post had been vacant since Lincoln removed McClellan from the general-in-chief role in March. McClellan remained commander of the Army of the Potomac."},{"label":"Halleck’s consultation","value":"July 24–27, 1862","note":"Halleck’s official report says that he left Washington on July 24, consulted McClellan at Harrison’s Landing, and returned on July 27. The episode describes him as arriving on July 24."},{"label":"Reinforcement sequence","value":"50,000 requested; 20,000 offered; 35,000 later stated as necessary","note":"Halleck reported that McClellan initially wanted at least 50,000 additional troops, agreed the next morning to attempt an advance with 20,000, and then raised the requirement to 35,000 after Halleck returned to Washington."},{"label":"Withdrawal order","value":"August 3, 1862, at 7:45 p.m.","note":"Halleck ordered McClellan to begin immediate measures to transfer the army from the Peninsula to Aquia Creek while covering the movement."},{"label":"Strategic purpose","value":"Unite McClellan’s army with Pope’s and cover Washington","note":"Halleck argued that the administration could not reinforce both separated armies and that Aquia Creek offered the feasible route to concentrate them on the Rappahannock line."},{"label":"Recommended book","value":"“Abraham Lincoln and the Second American Revolution” by James M. McPherson","note":"The podcast’s official book list identifies this collection of essays as the recommendation for episode 168."},{"label":"Transcript normalizations","value":"Benjamin Huger, Philip Kearny, Lafayette McLaws, William H. C. Whiting, George Brinton McClellan, and Gideon Welles","note":"The automated transcript produces “U.J.,” “Kearney,” “McClaws,” “Whiting” without initials, “George Britton McClellan,” and “Wells.” “U.J.” refers to Huger."}],"maps":[{"title":"From Malvern Hill to Harrison’s Landing","caption":"After the July 1 defensive victory, the Army of the Potomac moved roughly eight miles down the James River and established an entrenched camp at Harrison’s Landing under naval protection. Shoreline, road, route, position, and distance are generalized. This map is schematic and not to scale.","source":"National Park Service, U.S. Army Center of Military History, Library of Congress maps, and episode transcript; see sources below.","image":"/images/ep168-malvern-to-harrisons.svg","alt":"Generalized schematic map showing the Army of the Potomac withdrawing from Malvern Hill to Harrison’s Landing on July 2, 1862."},{"title":"Strategic choices after the Seven Days","caption":"McClellan proposed renewed operations against Richmond, including a possible movement toward Petersburg, but required reinforcements. Halleck instead ordered the army to move through Fort Monroe toward Aquia Creek so it could join Pope’s Army of Virginia and help cover Washington. Locations, waterways, routes, positions, and distances are generalized. This map is schematic and not to scale.","source":"Halleck’s official report, U.S. Army campaign histories, Library of Congress maps, and episode transcript; see sources below.","image":"/images/ep168-strategic-options.svg","alt":"Generalized schematic map of Washington, Aquia Creek, Pope’s position, Richmond, Petersburg, Fort Monroe, and McClellan at Harrison’s Landing."}],"moments":[{"time":"1:59","audioTime":119,"title":"The episode returns to Malvern Hill","detail":"The narrative begins by reviewing the final battle of the Seven Days and the Confederate defeat on July 1."},{"time":"3:13","audioTime":193,"title":"D. H. Hill identifies piecemeal attacks","detail":"The episode accepts Hill’s criticism that Confederate units entered separately but rejects his claim that Magruder’s command did little fighting."},{"time":"3:54","audioTime":234,"title":"Jackson’s role is reassessed","detail":"D. H. Hill’s division attacked under Jackson’s authority, and additional divisions were moving forward when darkness ended the battle."},{"time":"4:16","audioTime":256,"title":"Huger’s brigades enter the account","detail":"The transcript’s “U.J.” is Benjamin Huger. The episode states that three of his four brigades eventually joined the fighting despite friction with Magruder."},{"time":"4:30","audioTime":270,"title":"Longstreet and the artillery plan","detail":"The episode notes criticism of Longstreet for encouraging the belief that two large Confederate artillery groupings could suppress the Federal guns."},{"time":"4:53","audioTime":293,"title":"Magruder’s conduct is reviewed","detail":"The hosts separate Magruder’s delayed arrival from his later attacks, which they describe as compliance with Lee’s repeated orders."},{"time":"5:23","audioTime":323,"title":"Two attack orders reach Magruder","detail":"One order came through Lee’s chief of staff, Robert H. Chilton, and another came from Lee through Captain A. G. Dickinson."},{"time":"6:06","audioTime":366,"title":"The hosts assign final responsibility to Lee","detail":"Their interpretation places command responsibility for Malvern Hill’s Confederate defeat on the army commander."},{"time":"6:18","audioTime":378,"title":"The attack signal was unclear","detail":"Chilton’s untimed order made the advance of Lewis Armistead’s brigade the signal for a general assault, but the episode states that Armistead was not informed of that role."},{"time":"6:43","audioTime":403,"title":"Lee leaves the main front","detail":"After deciding that the artillery effort was failing, Lee moved away without first canceling the earlier assault instructions."},{"time":"6:57","audioTime":417,"title":"Reports prompt another attack order","detail":"Messages from Whiting and Magruder indicating Federal withdrawal and Armistead’s advance led Lee to order Magruder forward without personally confirming conditions at the front."},{"time":"7:15","audioTime":435,"title":"Lee continues the attacks","detail":"The episode states that Lee sent Lafayette McLaws’s division forward and redirected Magruder rather than stopping the assault."},{"time":"7:51","audioTime":471,"title":"Lee’s physical condition is discussed","detail":"The hosts describe him as fatigued and ill and note that Longstreet accompanied him in case he needed help directing the battle."},{"time":"9:09","audioTime":549,"title":"Confederates expect a counterattack","detail":"After the costly repulse, some Confederate officers expected McClellan to use Malvern Hill as the base for an attack on July 2."},{"time":"9:22","audioTime":562,"title":"Porter urges offensive action","detail":"Fitz John Porter and other Federal officers pressed McClellan to counterattack from the strong position."},{"time":"9:35","audioTime":575,"title":"McClellan orders the withdrawal to continue","detail":"The Army of the Potomac moved approximately eight miles down the James River to Harrison’s Landing."},{"time":"10:28","audioTime":628,"title":"McClellan estimates 180,000 Confederates","detail":"The episode presents the estimate as a large exaggeration that helped convince him continued retreat was necessary."},{"time":"11:23","audioTime":683,"title":"McClellan’s absence from battle is criticized","detail":"The narrative turns from the retreat decision to his repeated physical distance from the principal fighting during the Seven Days."},{"time":"12:26","audioTime":746,"title":"Corps commanders manage the retreat","detail":"The episode argues that McClellan left major battlefield decisions and the protection of the withdrawal to subordinate commanders."},{"time":"12:37","audioTime":757,"title":"Kearny condemns McClellan","detail":"Philip Kearny’s reported anger after the withdrawal is used to show the depth of criticism among some senior Federal officers."},{"time":"13:04","audioTime":784,"title":"The hosts offer a reputation-based explanation","detail":"They reject cowardice and treason and argue that McClellan distanced himself to protect his self-image and public reputation. This is their interpretation."},{"time":"14:26","audioTime":866,"title":"Army loyalty survives the retreat","detail":"The episode distinguishes dissatisfied officers from many soldiers who retained confidence in McClellan and blamed Washington for inadequate support."},{"time":"16:18","audioTime":978,"title":"Lincoln travels to Harrison’s Landing","detail":"On July 7, the president left Washington to inspect the Army of the Potomac and meet its commander."},{"time":"16:27","audioTime":987,"title":"McClellan gives Lincoln a policy letter","detail":"The Harrison’s Landing letter addressed national war policy as well as military operations."},{"time":"17:04","audioTime":1024,"title":"McClellan advocates a limited war","detail":"He argued for conciliation and against confiscation and forcible abolition as instruments of the war."},{"time":"17:21","audioTime":1041,"title":"Lincoln reads the letter without comment","detail":"The episode describes the president reading it in McClellan’s presence and pocketing it without giving the favorable response McClellan wanted."},{"time":"18:07","audioTime":1087,"title":"Federal policy is moving beyond conciliation","detail":"Congress was completing the Second Confiscation Act while Lincoln considered emancipation as a military measure."},{"time":"18:22","audioTime":1102,"title":"Lincoln consults Seward and Welles","detail":"The episode refers to Lincoln’s mid-July discussion with the secretaries of state and the navy about emancipation as a military necessity."},{"time":"18:45","audioTime":1125,"title":"The cabinet hears the emancipation draft","detail":"Lincoln presented his preliminary draft on July 22 and stated that the basic decision was his, while inviting cabinet advice on implementation and timing."},{"time":"19:16","audioTime":1156,"title":"Halleck becomes general-in-chief","detail":"Lincoln called Halleck from the Western Theater on July 11 to fill the post removed from McClellan in March."},{"time":"19:40","audioTime":1180,"title":"McClellan resents Halleck’s appointment","detail":"The episode quotes McClellan’s private description of the promotion as a personal rebuke and shows his contempt for civilian and military superiors."},{"time":"20:30","audioTime":1230,"title":"Halleck goes to Harrison’s Landing","detail":"He inspected the army and asked whether a practical renewed offensive against Richmond could begin from the James River base."},{"time":"21:29","audioTime":1289,"title":"McClellan asks for 50,000 reinforcements","detail":"Halleck’s official report and the episode agree that this was McClellan’s initial minimum for renewed operations."},{"time":"21:44","audioTime":1304,"title":"A movement against Petersburg is proposed","detail":"McClellan first suggested crossing the James and operating against Petersburg, south of Richmond."},{"time":"22:02","audioTime":1322,"title":"The proposal shifts back toward Richmond","detail":"McClellan then discussed advancing on the north bank of the James and stated that he needed 30,000 reinforcements."},{"time":"22:18","audioTime":1338,"title":"Halleck limits the offer to 20,000","detail":"Halleck said that Washington could not provide 50,000 and that he was authorized to offer no more than 20,000."},{"time":"22:47","audioTime":1367,"title":"McClellan accepts the smaller reinforcement","detail":"After considering the choice overnight, he told Halleck that he would attempt an advance with 20,000 additional soldiers."},{"time":"22:57","audioTime":1377,"title":"The stated requirement rises to 35,000","detail":"After Halleck returned to Washington, McClellan telegraphed that 35,000 reinforcements would be necessary."},{"time":"23:11","audioTime":1391,"title":"Halleck orders the army off the Peninsula","detail":"The August 3 telegram directed McClellan to begin the transfer to Aquia Creek immediately and cover the movement."},{"time":"23:30","audioTime":1410,"title":"The episode recommends James M. McPherson","detail":"The selection is “Abraham Lincoln and the Second American Revolution,” a collection of essays on Lincoln and the transformation produced by the war."}],"command":[{"side":"United States national and army command","color":"#2f5f9f","branches":[{"commander":"President Abraham Lincoln","command":"Commander in chief and head of the administration","subordinates":"Secretary of War Edwin M. Stanton; Secretary of State William H. Seward; Secretary of the Navy Gideon Welles"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Henry W. Halleck","command":"General-in-chief of the United States Army from July 11, 1862","subordinates":"Issued strategic instructions to McClellan and coordinated the intended concentration with Pope"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. George B. McClellan","command":"Army of the Potomac","subordinates":"Maj. Gen. Fitz John Porter, V Corps; other corps commanders managing the Seven Days withdrawal"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. John Pope","command":"Army of Virginia in northern Virginia","subordinates":"The force Halleck intended McClellan’s transferred army to reinforce and join"}]},{"side":"Confederate national and army command at Malvern Hill","color":"#a24a42","branches":[{"commander":"President Jefferson Davis","command":"Confederate commander in chief","subordinates":"Observed part of the July 1 operations and remained at the top of the Confederate political command"},{"commander":"Gen. Robert E. Lee","command":"Army of Northern Virginia","subordinates":"Chief of staff Col. Robert H. Chilton; Capt. A. G. Dickinson carried a direct order to Magruder"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. James Longstreet","command":"Senior division commander and adviser near Lee","subordinates":"Accompanied Lee during the battle and helped develop the artillery concept discussed in the episode"},{"commander":"Principal division commanders","command":"Malvern Hill attacking force","subordinates":"Thomas J. Jackson; John B. Magruder; Benjamin Huger; D. H. Hill; William H. C. Whiting; Lafayette McLaws"}]}],"people":[{"name":"George B. McClellan","role":"Commander of the Army of the Potomac who withdrew to Harrison’s Landing, advised Lincoln on war policy, and sought reinforcements for another advance","side":"United States","image":"/images/ep168-george-mcclellan.jpg"},{"name":"Abraham Lincoln","role":"President and commander in chief who inspected the army, received McClellan’s policy letter, and selected Halleck as general-in-chief","side":"United States"},{"name":"Henry W. Halleck","role":"New general-in-chief who assessed the Harrison’s Landing position and ordered the Army of the Potomac transferred to Aquia Creek","side":"United States","image":"/images/ep168-henry-halleck.jpg"},{"name":"Edwin M. Stanton","role":"Secretary of war and a principal civilian superior whom McClellan blamed for inadequate support","side":"United States"},{"name":"William H. Seward","role":"Secretary of state whom Lincoln consulted about emancipation in July 1862","side":"United States"},{"name":"Gideon Welles","role":"Secretary of the navy whom Lincoln consulted about emancipation; his department’s gunboats protected the Harrison’s Landing base","side":"United States"},{"name":"Fitz John Porter","role":"V Corps commander and McClellan ally who urged a counterattack after Malvern Hill","side":"United States"},{"name":"Philip Kearny","role":"Division commander whose anger at the withdrawal represented criticism of McClellan within the army","side":"United States"},{"name":"John Pope","role":"Commander of the Army of Virginia whose exposed position helped drive Halleck’s effort to unite the Federal armies","side":"United States"},{"name":"Robert E. Lee","role":"Commander of the Army of Northern Virginia and, in the hosts’ assessment, the officer bearing final responsibility for the Malvern Hill attacks","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"James Longstreet","role":"Division commander who advised Lee during the battle and accompanied him because Lee was unwell","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"John B. Magruder","role":"Division commander who attacked after receiving orders through Chilton and Dickinson","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Thomas J. “Stonewall” Jackson","role":"Wing commander whose available divisions entered the Malvern Hill action late and incompletely","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"D. H. Hill","role":"Division commander who criticized the piecemeal attacks and Magruder’s role after the battle","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Benjamin Huger","role":"Division commander whose cooperation with Magruder was slow; three of his four brigades joined the battle in the episode’s account","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Robert H. Chilton","role":"Lee’s chief of staff who transmitted the poorly timed and ambiguous order making Armistead’s advance the attack signal","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Lewis A. Armistead","role":"Brigade commander whose forward movement was named as the signal for a general assault without his being informed of that function","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Jefferson Davis","role":"Confederate president who was present near Lee while the army commander rested during the July 1 operations","side":"Confederacy"}],"losses":[],"gallery":[{"image":"/images/ep168-waud-harrisons-line.jpg","alt":"Alfred Waud drawing of the fortified Federal line at Harrison’s Landing with soldiers, artillery, earthworks, and tents.","caption":"Alfred R. Waud drew the Harrison’s Landing line in July or August 1862. The long, narrow field sketch records the defensive camp occupied after the Seven Days.","source":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below."},{"image":"/images/ep168-harrisons-landing-headquarters.jpg","alt":"Nine United States soldiers and an African American cook posed in front of the Fifth Corps headquarters tent at Harrison’s Landing.","caption":"The Fifth Corps headquarters at Harrison’s Landing, photographed in August 1862. Fitz John Porter is among the identified officers; the Library of Congress catalog also identifies the Black man at the left as the headquarters cook.","source":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below."},{"image":"/images/ep168-george-mcclellan.jpg","alt":"Wartime glass-plate portrait of Major General George B. McClellan in United States Army uniform.","caption":"George B. McClellan commanded the Army of the Potomac throughout the Peninsula Campaign and remained personally popular with many of its soldiers despite criticism of his battlefield conduct and strategic decisions.","source":"Library of Congress Civil War photograph collection; see sources below."},{"image":"/images/ep168-henry-halleck.jpg","alt":"Wartime glass-plate portrait of Major General Henry W. Halleck in United States Army uniform.","caption":"Henry W. Halleck became general-in-chief in July 1862. His Harrison’s Landing consultation and August 3 telegram ended the administration’s attempt to continue the Peninsula approach.","source":"Library of Congress Civil War photograph collection; see sources below."}],"sources":[{"label":"Official Civil War Podcast episode 168 page","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/2016/10/16/civil-war-podcast-episode-168/"},{"label":"Official Civil War Podcast book recommendations","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/book-recommendations/"},{"label":"National Park Service: Battle of Malvern Hill","url":"https://www.nps.gov/civilwar/search-battles-detail.htm?battleCode=va021"},{"label":"Richmond National Battlefield Park: The Battle of Malvern Hill","url":"https://www.nps.gov/rich/learn/historyculture/mhbull.htm"},{"label":"U.S. Army Center of Military History: Civil War campaign summary","url":"https://history.army.mil/Research/Reference-Topics/Army-Campaigns/Brief-Summaries/Civil-War/"},{"label":"U.S. Army Center of Military History: The Virginia Campaigns, March–August 1862","url":"https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/GOVPUB-D114-PURL-gpo72995/pdf/GOVPUB-D114-PURL-gpo72995.pdf"},{"label":"Official Records: McClellan report and Halleck correspondence, August 1862","url":"https://www.historyofwar.org/sources/acw/officialrecords/vol011chap023part1/00001_p3_09.html"},{"label":"Library of Congress: McClellan papers timeline","url":"https://www.loc.gov/collections/george-brinton-mcclellan-papers/articles-and-essays/timeline/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: July 7 Harrison’s Landing letter cover","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/mal1685600/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Lincoln and emancipation","url":"https://www.loc.gov/collections/abraham-lincoln-papers/articles-and-essays/abraham-lincoln-and-emancipation/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: July 22 preliminary emancipation draft","url":"https://www.loc.gov/resource/mal.1723200/?st=text"},{"label":"National Archives: Black soldiers and the July 1862 legislation","url":"https://www.archives.gov/education/lessons/blacks-civil-war"},{"label":"National Park Service: George B. McClellan","url":"https://www.nps.gov/people/george-b-mcclellan.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: Henry W. Halleck","url":"https://www.nps.gov/people/henry-w-halleck.htm"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Harrison’s Landing line by Alfred Waud","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2004660424/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Fifth Corps headquarters at Harrison’s Landing","url":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/98519364/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: George B. McClellan portrait","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018669440/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Henry W. Halleck portrait","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018666431/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Harrison’s Landing entrenchment map","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/gvhs01.vhs00295/"}]}}]}]
